# Proton Mail web-composer handoff

## Status

Accepted on 2026-09-30 from pinned Proton WebClients behavior.

## Decision

`protonmail-ai` may generate Proton Mail's web-composer `mailto:` handoff as a
local, no-send utility. This path is independent of Bridge and independent of
the optional direct API/session-fork client-identity blocker.

The command emits a URL only. It does not open a browser, authenticate, create a
draft through provider APIs, or send mail. The user opens the URL in an already
signed-in Proton Mail web session and reviews the resulting composer.

## Pinned evidence

At Proton WebClients revision
`74773f0b10cc31d32e43e82c061da5d75fef96ce`:

- `applications/mail/src/app/bootstrap.ts` registers Mail as a Chromium
  `mailto:` protocol handler;
- `applications/mail/src/app/constants.ts` defines the handler path as
  `/inbox/#mailto=%s`;
- `applications/mail/src/app/hooks/useMailtoHash.ts` accepts both raw and
  percent-encoded handoff values and guards against duplicate reopening;
- `applications/mail/src/app/helpers/url.ts` parses the primary recipient plus
  `subject`, `cc`, `bcc`, and `body` fields;
- `applications/mail/src/app/containers/ComposeProvider.tsx` turns the parsed
  handoff into a new-message composer action.

A current-main verification on 2026-09-30 at WebClients commit
`c571cd48b27ffcbc37b122eeaac4ee5233f532c4` found identical Git blob IDs for
`useMailtoHash.ts`, `helpers/url.ts`, `constants.ts`, and
`ComposeProvider.tsx`. The pinned behavior used here therefore still matches
current upstream for those critical files.

The parser deliberately does not translate `+` into a space before recipient
handling, which preserves tagged email aliases. Body line breaks are converted
for the HTML composer after URL decoding, and imported values pass through the
WebClients sanitization path.

## Encoding contract

The CLI first constructs an RFC 6068-style `mailto:` URI with each recipient or
query value percent-encoded. It then percent-encodes that complete URI for the
`#mailto=` handoff. The two layers are intentional: WebClients first decodes the
hash handoff and later decodes the individual mailto fields.

Tests cover tagged aliases, Unicode subject/body text, Cc/Bcc, line breaks,
missing recipients, duplicate flags, and body-file input.

## Privacy and side effects

Recipients, subject, and body are message content. They may appear in the
emitted URL and subsequently in browser history, terminal output, or clipboard
history. The generated URL must therefore never be treated as safe diagnostic
text.

`--body-file` avoids putting a long body directly in process arguments, but the
resulting URL still contains that body. The handoff never implies delivery;
reviewing and sending remain distinct side-effect boundaries.
